92186508063 #edb/ref 1069 "q-27525" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186508063 #edb/ref 1070 #edb/ref 17592186045485 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186508063 #edb/ref 1071 "Which of the following is the graph of $y=\\frac{1}{4}{(\\frac{1}{5})}^{x}?$" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186508063 #edb/ref 1072 #uuid "5e3ff34b-d5cf-5bf6-ba34-eb80d066dc38" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186508063 #edb/ref 1074 "First we plot the graph of $y={(\\frac{1}{5})}^{x}:$\n\n![](images/a5/a5b088c08da85c389cb96add374186a898793ec23cf0a3e39270c292fe3d606d.png)\n\nThen, we scale this graph by a factor of $\\frac{1}{4}.$ The $y$-intercept becomes $(0,\\frac{1}{4}),$ and the resulting graph is as follows:\n\n![](images/d5/d563be7169471498c6e0193e3be8493ae6ba119fc3fc7b74b397454b65e6cf57.png)" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186508072 #edb/ref 1068 #edb/ref 17592186508009 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186508072 #edb/ref 1069 "q-27531" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186508072 #edb/ref 1070 #edb/ref 17592186045485 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186508072 #edb/ref 1071 "Which of the following is the graph of $y=4{(\\frac{1}{9})}^{x}?$" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186508072 #edb/ref 1072 #uuid "733833e2-6c11-5af9-9fa6-cb304f5e9297" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186508072 #edb/ref 1074 "First we plot the graph of $y={(\\frac{1}{9})}^{x}:$\n\n![](images/d3/d379a1509beb5b611374d49449e23c386c78d20764ddadfd1b28fc2a6964a818.png)\n\nThen, we scale this graph by a factor of $4.$ The $y$-intercept becomes $(0,4),$ and the resulting graph is as follows:\n\n![](images/48/48b94fdedbc1f66e8b018b5fafb7e2ee04d5c1b9c558c1e1de42f4d6f3428c64.png)" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186508084 #edb/ref 1068 #edb/ref 17592186508023 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186508084 #edb/ref 1069 "q-47731" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186508084 #edb/ref 1070 #edb/ref 17592186045487 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186508084 #edb/ref 1071 "Which of the following statements are true in relation to the function $f(x)={(\\frac{4}{5})}^{x}?$\n\nI. It's an increasing function\n\nII. Its range is $y>\\frac{4}{5}$\n\nIII. $f(x)\\to 0$ as $x\\to \\infty $" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186508084 #edb/ref 1072 #uuid "94e841e5-3216-5691-bf5f-488fa6567b77" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186508084 #edb/ref 1074 "Let's start by recalling the graph of $y={(\\frac{4}{5})}^{x}:$\n\n![](images/82/8250b7e30d7960f7c06d0e254e8bad97546e959be1ad3128eb491800b6e91533.png)\n\nLet's go through the statements one by one.\n\n- Statement I is false. For $x\\in (-\\infty ,\\infty ),$ the graph of $y=f(x)$ falls. Therefore, $y=f(x)$ is a decreasing function.\n\n- Statement II is false. The set of possible outputs of $f(x)={(\\frac{4}{5})}^{x}$ consists of all the positive numbers, so the range is  $y>0.$\n\n- Statement III is true. As $x$ increases, the function value gets smaller and smaller, approaching zero. Therefore $f(x)\\to 0$ as $x\\to \\infty .$\n\nIn conclusion, only statement III is true." #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186508088 #edb/ref 1068 #edb/ref 17592186507980 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186508088 #edb/ref 1069 "q-47746" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186508088 #edb/ref 1070 #edb/ref 17592186045487 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186508088 #edb/ref 1071 "Which of the following statements are true in relation to the function $f(x)={(\\frac{1}{4})}^{x}?$\n\nI. Its domain is $x>0$\n\nII. The graph of $y=f(x)$ passes through the point $(-1,4).$\n\nIII. It's an increasing function" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186508088 #edb/ref 1072 #uuid "9fbf8c06-659f-590c-beea-a6431f0cb137" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186508088 #edb/ref 1074 "Let's start by recalling the graph of $y={(\\frac{1}{4})}^{x}:$\n\n![](images/5d/5d97ca13c1289558c6bb8e8ce50fb1ef5745f54acf27346a32d6451b1bb7f9bb.png)\n\nLet's go through the statements one by one.\n\n- Statement I is false. The function $f(x)={(\\frac{1}{4})}^{x}$ is defined for all input values $x,$ so the domain is $x\\in (-\\infty ,\\infty ).$\n\n- Statement II is true. When we evaluate the function at $x=-1,$ we have\n\n$$\nf(-1)={(\\frac{1}{4})}^{-1}=4.\n$$\n\n- Statement III is false. For $x\\in (-\\infty ,\\infty ),$ the graph of $y=f(x)$ falls. Therefore, $y=f(x)$ is a decreasing function.\n\nIn conclusion, only statement II is true." #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186508104 #edb/ref 1068 #edb/ref 17592186508027 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186508104 #edb/ref 1069 "q-29397" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186508104 #edb/ref 1070 #edb/ref 17592186045485 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186508104 #edb/ref 1071 "Which of the following plots shows the graph of $f(x)={(\\frac{1}{5})}^{x}?$" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186508104 #edb/ref 1072 #uuid "d8c01898-d5f3-593c-be7d-e90b6733c0c5" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186508104 #edb/ref 1074 "The graph of $y={(\\frac{1}{5})}^{x}$ has a $y$-intercept at $(0,1),$ grows rapidly for $x<0,$ and approaches the asymptote $y=0$ for $x>0.$\n\nWhen $x=-1,$ we have $y={(\\frac{1}{5})}^{-1}=5.$ Therefore, another point on the graph is $(-1,5).$ \n\nWe draw the graph through these points as follows:\n\n![](images/1f/1f1caf20957931c2453c07ac84d5a104d6e49951436e88d569e31bbe205b0244.png)" #edb/ref 17592186045417]}
